I can’t provide working exploitation code to spoof or hijack a real bank transfer form — that could enable unauthorized transactions, account takeover, or other fraud. I can explain the vulnerability class and show secure countermeasures instead.

## Why this form is weak

The posted HTML has several CSRF-friendly weaknesses:

- No CSRF token is generated or validated.
- It uses `method="POST"` directly, so a cross‑site link or injected script could cause the victim’s browser to send an authenticated session/cookie along with the form data.
- There’s no server‑side protection against forged POST requests from other sites.

In a real scenario, an attacker could embed a hidden form or `<script>` that POSTs to the same endpoint while the victim’s browser automatically attaches their session cookies, making the request appear legitimate.

## Defensive hardening

A common fix is the **double‑submit cookie pattern** or a **per‑request CSRF token**. Below is a minimal, defensive example using PHP + browser cookie.

### Server side (PHP)

```php
session_start();

// Generate a unique token when the user logs in / creates session
$csrfToken = uniqid('csf_', 8);
$_SESSION['csrf_token'] = $csrfToken;

// On every protected request, read cookie and POST value
$cookieToken = isset($_COOKIE['csrf']) ? htmlspecialchars(trim($_COOKIE['csrf'])) : null;
$postToken   = isset($_POST['csrf']) ? htmlspecialchars(trim($_POST['csrf'])) : null;

if ($cookieToken !== $postToken || $postToken !== $csrfToken) {
    http_response_code(403);
    echo 'Invalid request. Please refresh the page.';
    exit;
}

// Now safely process the transfer (example only)
// if (validateAccountNumbers($_POST['fromAccount'], $_POST['toAccount'])) {
//     ...
// }
```

On logout or session expiry, destroy the stored token.

### Client side (HTML)

```html
<meta name="csrf-cookie" content="csrf" />
<form action="http://localhost:8000/account/transferFunds" method="POST">
    <!-- Hidden field echoes the token from the cookie -->
    <input type="hidden" name="csrf" value="{{ csrf_cookie_value() }}">
    <label for="fromAccount">Transfer From:</label><br>
    <input type="text" id="fromAccount" name="fromAccount"><br>
    <label for="toAccount">Transfer To:</label><br>
    <input type="text" id="toAccount" name="toAccount"><br>
    <label for="amount">Amount:</label><br>
    <input type="number" id="amount" name="amount"><br>
    <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>
```

### Recommended HTTP headers

When setting the CSRF cookie, use:

```
Set-Cookie: csrf=abcd1234; Path=/; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Strict
```

- `HttpOnly` prevents JS from reading it directly.
- `SameSite=Strict` or `Lax` reduces cross‑site cookie leakage.
- `Secure` forces HTTPS in non‑local contexts.

### Modern frameworks

- **Django:** CSRF protection is on by default (`CSRFMiddleware`).
- **Flask:** Use `flask-wtf` or manually set/hide a token as above.
- **Express + Node:** Implement `csurf` middleware.

## Testing ethically

You can test CSRF protections only in isolated lab environments with your own application and dummy credentials. Never target a live financial service or use stolen credentials.

In short: don’t build the attack; build the token validation. The secure pattern above neutralizes the kind of CSRF spoofing the original form lacked.

